# Grip Shot / Amazon Seller SaaS

## Stack
pnpm monorepo, TypeScript, Next.js App Router, Firebase Auth, Firestore, Cloud Storage, Stripe Billing, Gemini API, Vitest, Playwright. `packages/workflow-core` is the image-generation domain: extend it, do not replace.

## How to work
- Feature-by-feature, small vertical slices; read existing code before adding new code.
- Each feature: code + tests + docs + README updates per repo conventions.
- App must run locally and deploy to Cloud Run. Stateless: user files only in managed storage.
- Use feature branches; merge only when tests pass. Commit messages: `Cloude: <msg>`.
- If requirements are ambiguous, choose the commercially sensible, technically robust path.

## Quality gates
Feature is done when: unit + integration tests pass; Playwright when UI changes; lint + typecheck pass; local run works; docs and README updated.

## Dependencies
Only well-known, actively maintained packages (>1k weekly downloads). Pin exact versions (no ^ or ~). Ask before adding new dependencies.

## Architecture
- Firebase Auth: email/password + Google; users belong to a workspace with subscription + quota.
- Stripe webhooks are the source of truth for billing state.
- Generation stops at quota; nothing already generated is lost. Prefer dry-run paths for expensive generation.
- Prepare backend abstractions for future WhatsApp/Telegram; do not block core launch on messaging.

## Autonomy
Do: small high-value improvements, validations, naming, security/observability where sensible.
Don't: replace the stack without strong justification, build large speculative systems first, silently remove behavior, ship fake implementations without labeling them.
